# The privacy tier. Never committed: a raw corpus is a person's data, and a copy of it
# in a public repository is the one mistake that cannot be taken back.
data/raw/

# Artifacts. Git tracks none of them; the run manifest records their digests, which
# is what makes a release identifiable without committing what it wrote. Listed per
# tier rather than as `data/`, so a tier can be un-ignored on its own. `run.json` is
# a file rather than a tier -- one run, one manifest, at the root of what it wrote.
data/interim/
data/processed/
data/release/
data/quarantine/
data/run.json
# Votes already paid for, keyed by everything that determines one (`edge/panel.py`). Not an
# artifact tier: it is the one thing under `data/` that is not what a run wrote but what a run
# may re-use, and a committed one would be a panel's answers about a corpus nobody has here.
data/cache/
*.jsonl
!tests/fixtures/**/*.jsonl

# Per-model LLM settings, read at the edge through `agent-toolkit`'s
# `JsonDirConfigResolver` (`<model>.json`, the convention it preserves). Never committed:
# a file naming an endpoint and holding a key does not belong in a public repository, and the
# library's own YAML resolver refuses both keys for the same reason. Nothing is committed in
# their place -- README names the shape one of these files takes.
config/model/*.json

# A developer's own SQLite file, where they pointed `DATAFORCE_DATABASE_URL` at one. Nothing
# defaults to it -- an unset DSN means *no store*, and a fallback file is the mistake nobody
# notices until a corpus is somewhere nobody chose. Ignored because a committed one would be
# real records in a public repository.
dataforce.sqlite3

.env
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
